---
name: vibemon-sprite-sheet
description: Generates a Pokémon-style sprite sheet for a Vibemon.
version: 1.0.0
---

Render ONE continuous image showing a single Pokémon-style creature named "{{ vibemon.affinity.identity.name }}" in three positions arranged horizontally across the canvas. The same creature appears in all three positions — identical silhouette, proportions, palette, and design features. Only the pose changes.

STYLE
- Pokémon Sugimori aesthetic: flat cel shading, clean confident lineart in #0A0A1A, soft gradient fills.
- No text, numbers, labels, borders, panel dividers, watermarks, or UI elements anywhere in the image.

CANVAS
- 21:9 ultrawide, split into three equal horizontal panels, seamless (no visible borders).
- ONE continuous background: flat matte {{ bg_hex }} filling the entire canvas edge to edge as a single uniform color. Do NOT render panel divisions, gutters, borders, seams, or any color variation across the canvas. The background is a single unbroken wash.
- No gradient, no vignette, no ground shadow, no floor line.

SUBJECT
- Name: {{ vibemon.affinity.identity.name }}
- Types: {{ vibemon.affinity.identity.elements | join(", ") }} — the silhouette, color palette, and accent details should read this typing at a glance.
- Design Tier BST: {{ vibemon.affinity.identity.bst }}
{% if vibemon.affinity.identity.visual_notes -%}
- Design notes: {{ vibemon.affinity.identity.visual_notes }}
{% endif %}

INFLUENCES (earlier entries dominate; later entries are accents).
Translate each influence into concrete visual language — shape, texture, color, material, motif, or motion. If an influence is abstract (a mood, a weather state, a concept), invent a specific visual metaphor for it rather than ignoring it. Every listed influence must be visible in the final design.
{% for affinity in vibemon.birth_affinities -%}
{% if affinity.visual_notes -%}
- {{ affinity.visual_notes }} ({{ affinity.intensity * 100 | round(2) }}%)
{% endif -%}
{% endfor %}

BASE ANATOMY
The creature's base body plan should not default to common "cute mammal" templates (cat, fox, dog, rabbit, bear). Draw from a diverse anatomical palette — plant, fungal, insect, arachnid, crustacean, mollusk, reptile, amphibian, avian, aquatic, elemental, or invented hybrid. Let the type(s) suggest non-obvious body plans: a grass creature can be a walking seedpod or a moss-covered amphibian, not just a leafy mammal.

Design tier reference (BST → visual language).
Each tier's complexity budget is a CEILING, not a target. Stay under it.

<400  runt — first-stage, ~1-2 ft tall
  COMPLEXITY BUDGET: 1 signature element total (pick one: a marking, a tuft, a small accessory, or a distinguishing feature — NOT more than one)
  SILHOUETTE: simple, rounded, readable at a glance — circle/oval/teardrop base
  PROPORTIONS: oversized head, short limbs, stubby or absent tail
  COLOR: 2-3 zones maximum
  POSTURE: neutral, curious, or low-to-ground
  PRESENCE: small, toy-like, approachable
  DO NOT: add capes, mantles, flowing elements, multiple accessories, glowing effects, ornate markings, armor, horns, crests, or "cool" features. This is a baby.

400-499  mid — second-stage, ~2-4 ft tall
  COMPLEXITY BUDGET: 2 signature elements total (e.g., a tail shape AND a head feature — but not also a cape, also markings, also accessories)
  SILHOUETTE: clearer limbs, distinguishable features, some asymmetry allowed
  PROPORTIONS: balanced; head-to-body ratio closer to realistic but still slightly cute
  COLOR: 3-4 zones maximum
  POSTURE: grounded and alert, confident but not imposing
  PRESENCE: capable but not impressive
  DO NOT: add capes, mantles, multi-layered features, flowing fur or hair, glowing auras, floral sprays, elaborate tails, or more than one accent per body region. This is an awkward teenager, not a final form.

500-569  solid — final-stage, ~4-6 ft tall
  COMPLEXITY BUDGET: 3-4 signature elements
  SILHOUETTE: sturdy, weighted, readable fighting stance even at rest
  PROPORTIONS: athletic or heavy-set; broad shoulders, planted legs, substantial tail
  COLOR: 4-5 zones
  POSTURE: grounded, ready, centered weight; focused expression
  PRESENCE: fully evolved, takes up space
  DO NOT: add glowing auras, floating elements, celestial motifs, or legendary framing. This is a strong creature, not a myth.

570-669  apex — pseudo-legendary, ~5-8 ft tall
  COMPLEXITY BUDGET: 5-6 signature elements, layered
  SILHOUETTE: dramatic and distinctive — readable in pure black
  PROPORTIONS: imposing; long limbs or elongated body, dynamic proportions, dramatic tail/wings/mane
  COLOR: 5-6 zones, layered accents, flowing elements (ribbons, flames, crystals)
  POSTURE: commanding, chest forward or wings spread; intense, eye contact with viewer
  PRESENCE: fills the frame, implies threat or majesty
  DO NOT: add halos, orbits, runic inscriptions, or weightless/floating anatomy — those belong to mythic.

670+  mythic — legendary, otherworldly scale
  COMPLEXITY BUDGET: unlimited, but must remain visually coherent
  SILHOUETTE: otherworldly — geometric, floating, or anatomically unusual (multiple tails, ringed body, no visible limbs)
  PROPORTIONS: break naturalism — impossibly long, cosmically small-headed, or weightless
  COLOR: 6+ zones, ornate and symbolic — glowing elements, celestial/ancient motifs (runes, halos, orbits, relics), metallic or gemlike surfaces
  POSTURE: levitating, coiled, or statuesque; unreadable or serene expression
  PRESENCE: singular, clearly the only one of its kind; energy the canvas can barely contain

SCENE FRAMING
The three positions together depict a single battle scene: the LEFT creature and the RIGHT creature are facing each other as opponents, with the CENTER pose as a hero presentation between them. This framing drives the pose geometry below.

POSES (left → center → right, same creature throughout)

1. LEFT — Full back view, facing its opponent on the right.
   The camera is positioned behind the creature. Only the back, rear, tail, and backs of the ears/wings/limbs are visible. The face is NOT visible — at most a tiny sliver of cheek or the back of the head. Body oriented toward the right side of the canvas, as if squaring up against the creature in the right position. Posture reactive, one limb or appendage raised in challenge. Think: a trainer's view of their own Pokémon from behind during a battle.

2. CENTER — Hero showcase pose, facing the viewer.
   Official Pokémon artwork style — the creature's flagship presentation image. Facing the camera directly (or at most a subtle 5-10° turn), full body visible, confident and charismatic expression. A signature gesture that communicates personality and typing — e.g. a paw raised, a flourish, a signature element (flame, leaf, spark) actively displayed. NOT a neutral at-rest pose. This is the creature's "cover of the box" moment.

3. RIGHT — Three-quarter combat stance, facing its opponent on the left.
   Body angled toward the left side of the canvas (toward the LEFT pose's back), camera sees a three-quarter front view. Posture aggressive and ready, appendages raised or spread, head tilted slightly down, intense expression. Intensity of the combat pose should match the design tier — restrained for runt, commanding for apex, reality-bending for mythic.

Adapt all pose language to the creature's actual anatomy. If it has no arms, no wings, or unusual anatomy (serpentine, blob, quadruped, floating), interpret "limbs raised," "signature gesture," or "squared up" accordingly. Do NOT add wings, arms, or features that aren't part of the established design.